WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.711
Part one projected onto a line.

2
00:00:05.802 --> 00:00:08.069
Now the drone can reach a

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.064
whole plane in space.

4
00:00:10.064 --> 00:00:12.876
We look at that plane face on

5
00:00:12.876 --> 00:00:14.780
from straight above.

6
00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.528
Its buttons u and v span the plane.

7
00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:21.343
Put them into the columns of

8
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.770
a three by two matrix A.

9
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.498
A customer waits at one, one, one.

10
00:00:27.498 --> 00:00:30.478
It hovers just off the plane

11
00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:32.167
above this spot

12
00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:36.240
one minus one plus one is one, not zero.

13
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.640
Now turn to a side view

14
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.862
the plane becomes a line

15
00:00:40.862 --> 00:00:43.596
and the customer sits above it.

16
00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:46.671
We want the gap to be perpendicular

17
00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:48.380
to the whole plane.

18
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.858
How many conditions is that?

19
00:00:50.858 --> 00:00:52.310
Pause and think.

20
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.031
Two conditions are enough.

21
00:00:58.031 --> 00:01:01.653
If the gap is perpendicular to u, and to v

22
00:01:01.736 --> 00:01:04.041
it is perpendicular to every

23
00:01:04.123 --> 00:01:05.770
combination of them.

24
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.965
Stack both conditions: A transpose

25
00:01:09.965 --> 00:01:13.480
times b minus A x hat, is zero.

26
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.953
Multiply out

27
00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:17.889
and you get the normal equations.

28
00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:20.307
Normal means perpendicular

29
00:01:20.307 --> 00:01:23.070
the gap is normal to the plane.

30
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.497
For the drone, A transpose A is six, nine

31
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:33.696
nine, fourteen, and A transpose b is four

32
00:01:33.696 --> 00:01:34.300
six.

33
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.944
The solution is two thirds, zero.

34
00:01:37.944 --> 00:01:42.037
So the closest point is two thirds of u

35
00:01:42.037 --> 00:01:45.730
two thirds, four thirds, two thirds.

36
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.861
The gap is one third of one, minus one, one.

37
00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:53.041
It meets the plane at a right angle

38
00:01:53.041 --> 00:01:54.760
in both directions.

39
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.083
Solve for x hat and multiply by A.

40
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:02.165
The projection matrix is A

41
00:02:02.165 --> 00:02:06.128
times the inverse of A transpose A

42
00:02:06.128 --> 00:02:08.220
times A transpose.

43
00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.245
For the drone, P has rank two

44
00:02:11.245 --> 00:02:14.010
everything lands in the plane.

45
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.559
Project twice and nothing changes

46
00:02:17.559 --> 00:02:19.440
and P is symmetric.

47
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.595
P depends only on the plane.

48
00:02:22.595 --> 00:02:25.900
Use another basis, like w and s

49
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.805
and you get the same matrix.

50
00:02:28.805 --> 00:02:31.910
Only the amounts x hat change.

51
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:35.311
But the columns must be independent.

52
00:02:35.311 --> 00:02:37.770
Put all four buttons into A

53
00:02:37.854 --> 00:02:39.973
and A transpose A is four

54
00:02:40.058 --> 00:02:42.940
by four with rank two: no inverse.

55
00:02:43.808 --> 00:02:45.137
A classic slip

56
00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:48.679
split the inverse of A transpose A into

57
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.957
A inverse times A transpose inverse.

58
00:02:51.957 --> 00:02:54.171
Then everything cancels

59
00:02:54.171 --> 00:02:56.208
and P is the identity.

60
00:02:56.788 --> 00:02:59.584
But the drone's A is three by two.

61
00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:01.723
It has no inverse at all

62
00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:04.108
so the split is not allowed.

63
00:03:04.458 --> 00:03:07.579
And P is clearly not the identity.

64
00:03:07.579 --> 00:03:11.618
It moves one, one, one down onto the plane.

65
00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.895
Only for a square invertible A is

66
00:03:14.984 --> 00:03:17.468
the column space everything.

67
00:03:17.468 --> 00:03:20.307
Then every target is reachable

68
00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:22.968
and P really is the identity.

69
00:03:23.548 --> 00:03:25.985
Now the promised proof that

70
00:03:26.075 --> 00:03:28.150
p is the closest point.

71
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.948
Take any point q of the plane.

72
00:03:31.298 --> 00:03:35.509
Cut a slice through b, p and q.

73
00:03:35.509 --> 00:03:40.942
b minus q is the gap e, plus p minus q

74
00:03:40.942 --> 00:03:44.338
which lies in the plane.

75
00:03:44.688 --> 00:03:47.859
The gap is perpendicular to the plane

76
00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:51.446
so these two pieces meet at a right angle.

77
00:03:51.446 --> 00:03:54.450
Pythagoras: the squared distance to

78
00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:56.286
q is the gap squared

79
00:03:56.286 --> 00:03:58.288
plus p minus q squared.

80
00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:01.264
So it can only be bigger.

81
00:04:01.264 --> 00:04:05.571
Check: at u the squared distance is one

82
00:04:05.571 --> 00:04:10.087
at v it is five, and at p it is one third

83
00:04:10.087 --> 00:04:11.558
the smallest.

84
00:04:11.908 --> 00:04:14.388
Minimising a sum of squares by

85
00:04:14.471 --> 00:04:17.281
projecting is exactly what Lecture

86
00:04:17.364 --> 00:04:19.348
fourteen does with data.

87
00:04:19.928 --> 00:04:22.916
I minus P keeps the other part

88
00:04:22.916 --> 00:04:25.134
it sends b to the gap.

89
00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.640
That is how you remove an

90
00:04:27.736 --> 00:04:29.568
unwanted direction.

91
00:04:29.918 --> 00:04:32.281
A well-known study of word embeddings

92
00:04:32.344 --> 00:04:34.643
removed a gender direction this way

93
00:04:34.643 --> 00:04:36.878
from words that should be neutral.

94
00:04:37.228 --> 00:04:39.601
A small made-up café example

95
00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:41.975
a recommender describes each

96
00:04:42.056 --> 00:04:44.266
menu item by three numbers

97
00:04:44.266 --> 00:04:46.557
and the direction one, one

98
00:04:46.557 --> 00:04:48.112
zero tracks price.

99
00:04:48.112 --> 00:04:50.404
To compare by flavour only

100
00:04:50.404 --> 00:04:53.268
keep the part perpendicular to it.

101
00:04:53.618 --> 00:04:56.605
Before, the gold latte and the truffle

102
00:04:56.683 --> 00:04:58.020
mocha look alike

103
00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.771
mostly because both are expensive.

104
00:05:00.771 --> 00:05:01.321
After

105
00:05:01.321 --> 00:05:04.858
their cosine is minus zero point five eight.

106
00:05:05.208 --> 00:05:06.606
Without its price

107
00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:09.947
the gold latte is almost the house coffee.

108
00:05:09.947 --> 00:05:13.288
The coffee and the iced tea barely change.

109
00:05:13.638 --> 00:05:16.594
One caution: only what lies along that

110
00:05:16.671 --> 00:05:18.305
direction is removed.

111
00:05:18.382 --> 00:05:20.638
Information spread over other

112
00:05:20.716 --> 00:05:22.038
directions stays.

113
00:05:24.618 --> 00:05:27.488
So, to project onto a subspace

114
00:05:27.488 --> 00:05:31.561
make the gap perpendicular to every column.

115
00:05:31.561 --> 00:05:34.338
That is the normal equations.

116
00:05:34.688 --> 00:05:37.059
The projection matrix needs

117
00:05:37.147 --> 00:05:38.903
independent columns

118
00:05:38.903 --> 00:05:41.888
and depends only on the subspace.

119
00:05:42.238 --> 00:05:43.820
And by Pythagoras

120
00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.598
no point of the subspace is closer than p.

121
00:05:48.178 --> 00:05:52.785
Try one: move the customer to three, six

122
00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:53.459
four.

123
00:05:53.459 --> 00:05:55.818
What is the gap now?

124
00:05:56.168 --> 00:05:57.284
Next lecture

125
00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:01.149
the same normal equations fit a line through

126
00:06:01.234 --> 00:06:04.240
data points that do not lie on one

127
00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.528
least squares.
